**Theorem 82.1 (the dyadic companion residue test).** Let $q,r$ be distinct primes with

$$q\equiv7\pmod{24},\qquad r\equiv13\pmod{24},\qquad (5/q)=(5/r)=(q/r)=1,$$

and put $D=qr$. There exist integers $u,v$ satisfying

$$q=u^2-5v^2,\qquad u>0,\quad u\equiv2\pmod4,\quad v\text{ odd}.$$

For every such representation, if an original golden block satisfies $B_N=L_N^2+3=D C^2$ with $N=3^j$ and positive integers $j,C$, then

$$\mathcal R(5,-D,q)=\mathcal R(5,q,-D)=-1,$$
$$\boxed{\left(\frac{u+vs}{r}\right)=-\left(\frac{2u}{q}\right)=-\left(\frac{u/2}{5}\right),\qquad s^2\equiv5\pmod r.}$$

The left-hand symbol is independent of the choice of $s$. Its opposite sign therefore excludes $D$ as the squarefree kernel for every $C$ and every ternary index.

**Proof.** We use [Stevenhagen, *Rédei reciprocity, governing fields, and negative Pell*](https://arxiv.org/pdf/1806.06250), Corollary 5.2, Definitions 7.5--7.8, Theorem 7.9, and equations (45)--(48), with the conventions of §81. The discriminants of $(5,-D,q)$ are $5,-D,4q$, whose three-way gcd is one. The pairs $(5,q)$ and $(5,-D)$ are locally split by the $5$-splitting conditions; their dyadic symbols are trivial because $5\equiv1\pmod4$. For $(-D,q)$, the factors at $q,r$ are $(r/q),(q/r)=1$, using reciprocity and $r\equiv1\pmod4$; its dyadic symbol is trivial because $-D\equiv1\pmod4$. All other finite places are unit pairs, and infinity is trivial. Thus the exact admissibility conditions include the overlapping argument $q$.

For an actual block, $\beta=-1-F_N\sqrt5$ has norm $-D C^2$, satisfies $\beta+\bar\beta=-2$, and lies in $1+4\mathcal O_0$, where $\mathcal O_0=\mathbb Z[(1+\sqrt5)/2]$. Here $F_N\equiv2\pmod4$ and $D\equiv3\pmod8$, exactly as in Theorem 81.1. Its complete finite-place proof therefore applies: valuations outside $5D$ are even; at $q,r$ use the conjugate unit $-2$, including when these primes divide $C$; at $5$ use a unit; at $2$ use the étale quadratic polynomial for an element of $1+4\mathcal O_0$. Thus $\mathbb Q(\sqrt5,\sqrt{-D},\sqrt\beta)$ is minimally ramified over $\mathbb Q(\sqrt{-5D})$. Evaluation at $q$ now gives

$$\mathcal R(5,-D,q)=(-2/q)=-1,$$

because $q\equiv7\pmod8$. Rédei reciprocity gives $\mathcal R(5,q,-D)=-1$.

For generator existence, the norm-Euclidean argument of Theorem 81.3 gives a generator $\pi$ of a prime above $q$ with norm $\pm q$. Multiplication by $\phi$ if needed makes its norm $q$. The group $(\mathcal O_0/2\mathcal O_0)^\times$ is cyclic of order three, generated by $\phi^2$. Multiply by a power of this norm-one unit to obtain $\pi\equiv1\pmod{2\mathcal O_0}$. Its two real signs agree, so multiplication by $-1$ if needed makes it totally positive and preserves that congruence. Write

$$\pi=1+2(m+n\phi)=u+v\sqrt5,\qquad u=1+2m+n,\quad v=n.$$

Then $u+v$ is odd. Since $q=u^2-5v^2\equiv7\pmod8$, $u$ must be even and $v$ odd; $u\equiv0\pmod4$ would give $q\equiv3\pmod8$, so $u\equiv2\pmod4$. Total positivity gives $u>0$. Primality gives $\gcd(u,v)=1$ and $q\nmid2u$.

Now take any representation in the statement, put $\gamma=u+v\sqrt5$, and define $E_q=\mathbb Q(\sqrt5,\sqrt q)$ and $F_q=E_q(\sqrt\gamma)$. Its odd-prime minimality over $K_q=\mathbb Q(\sqrt{5q})$ follows from the same norm-$q$ argument as in Theorem 81.2. Away from $5q$, $\gamma$ is a unit; at $5$ it is a unit in the ramified quadratic field; at $q$ use the conjugate unit $2u$, since $E_q(\sqrt\gamma)=E_q(\sqrt{\bar\gamma})$.

The dyadic condition is different. Here $\Delta(q)=4q\equiv4\pmod8$ and $\Delta(5)=5$, so Definition 7.6 requires the $2$-minimal ramification of Definition 7.5. Corollary 5.2 supplies the equivalent norm element $\alpha=2(u+\sqrt q)$ in $\mathbb Q(\sqrt q)$. Since $q\equiv7\pmod8$, choose odd $t\in\mathbb Z_2$ with $t^2=-q$ and write $\sqrt q=it$. Locally $E_q=\mathbb Q_2(i,\sqrt5)$, and

$$\frac{\alpha}{(1+i)^2}=t-iu\in1+2\mathbb Z_2[i],\qquad N(t-iu)=t^2+u^2=5v^2.$$

The denominator is a square. This is precisely the $2$-minimal condition in Definition 7.5 and its following explanation, with $a=q\equiv-1\pmod8$ and $b=5$. No unramified-at-two assertion is substituted for this condition.

This minimally ramified field computes $\mathcal R(5,q,-D)$. Total positivity of $\gamma$ makes the infinite factor $+1$. The finite factors at $q,r$ are $(2u/q)$ and $(u+vs/r)$, respectively. Both residues are nonzero. The two roots of five at $r$ give equal characters because the product of the residues is $q$ and $(q/r)=1$. Hence

$$-1=\mathcal R(5,q,-D)=\left(\frac{2u}{q}\right)\left(\frac{u+vs}{r}\right).$$

Finally write $u=2w$, with $w>0$ odd. Quadratic reciprocity for Jacobi symbols gives

$$\left(\frac{2u}{q}\right)=\left(\frac{w}{q}\right)=(-1)^{(w-1)/2}\left(\frac{q}{w}\right)=(-1)^{(w-1)/2}\left(\frac{-5v^2}{w}\right)=\left(\frac{w}{5}\right).$$

Coprimality removes $v^2$, and the reciprocity sign from $q\equiv3\pmod4$ cancels $(-1/w)$. The convention for $w=1$ gives the same equality. This proves the boxed test. ∎

Together with §81, this supplies explicit fixed-golden-field tests in both mutual-residue types. The theorem retains the original integral block hypothesis and all prime/splitting assumptions. No independence from every earlier Cassels or support condition, previously surviving numerical pair, prime-family infinitude, or universal WSS exclusion is asserted.

